{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.RothOperatorFamily","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.RothOperatorFamily","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.RothInductionStep","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.BoundedOrderWronskian","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.SeparatedRepresentation","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.RothLemmaStatement"],"declarations":24,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_le_of_rothGrowth_of_rescaled_bounds","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_le_of_rothGrowth_of_rescaled_bounds","kind":"theorem","line":869,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothOperatorFamily.lean#L869-L907","doc":"**§13's conclusion from bounds at the rescaled degrees** — the literal shape of the\ninduction hypothesis.\n\n`RothLemmaUniform` bounds the index of a polynomial *at its own declared degrees*.  In the\ninduction step the sub-problems carry the rescaled degrees `r·d`, so what the hypothesis\nsupplies is `hres`, not `hfac`.  `factor_bound_of_rescaled` converts one into the other, and\nthe factor `r` it introduces is exactly the one §13's `S/r` cancels.\n\n⚠ `hres` is still an assumption.  Discharging it is the induction: the `x` side from\n`RothLemmaUniform` at `m−1` (degree and height bounds on the Wronskian exist —\n`degreeOf_mvGenWronskian_le`, `abs_coeff_mvGenWronskian_le`), the `y` side from\n`RothBaseCase`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_le_of_rothGrowth_of_factor_bound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_le_of_rothGrowth_of_factor_bound","kind":"theorem","line":833,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothOperatorFamily.lean#L833-L866","doc":"**§13's conclusion in the form the recursion consumes.**\n\n`hfac` is what the induction hypothesis supplies: for *any* bounded-order family realising a\nseparated representation of `P`, the two Wronskians' indices sum to at most `r·Φ`.  Then\n\n    index P ≤ 1/ω + 2·max(Φ, q)        whenever  (d+1)·Φ ≤ q²·D₂.\n\nBoth `1/ω` and `Φ` are small in the intended regime, and neither mentions the representation\nlength — the `r` in `hfac` cancels against the `S/r` in §13's conclusion.\n\n⚠ Still not Roth's lemma: `hfac` is an assumption here, and discharging it is the induction. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_family_index_le_w_of_rothGrowth","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_family_index_le_w_of_rothGrowth","kind":"theorem","line":750,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothOperatorFamily.lean#L750-L831","doc":"**§13's conclusion in `RothLemma4`'s own shape** — the two branches, packaged as `w`, with\nthe realising family handed back and **no `q` and no side condition**.\n\n`index_le_two_mul_max_of_det_bound` rounds the two branches into `t + 2·max(Φ, q)`, which costs\na rational stand-in `q` for `√Φ` and the junction condition `(d+1)·Φ ≤ q²·D₂`.  That condition\nis where the `1/d` slack lives, and it is fatal here: the smallest admissible `q` has\n`q² = (1 + 1/d)·Φ`, so `q² ≤ Ψ` fails by exactly `Φ/d` and `RothLemma4`'s `w` cannot be `q`.\n\n`index_le_or_sq_of_det_bound` keeps the branches apart and squared, and then neither problem\narises:\n\n* `θ − t ≤ 2Φ`      → `w := Φ`, first disjunct;\n* `(θ − t)²·d ≤ 2rΦ` → `(θ − t)² ≤ 4Φ` by `r ≤ d + 1 ≤ 2d`, so `w := max(0, (θ − t)/2)` has\n  `w² ≤ Φ`, second disjunct.\n\nThe `max(0, ·)` is what makes the second branch unconditional: when `θ < t` the halved\ndifference is negative and `w := 0` serves, needing only `0 ≤ Φ`.\n\n⚠ The degree floor is `d₂ ()` itself, not a parameter `D₂ ≤ d₂ ()`.  The second branch divides\nby it, so a smaller floor would weaken `r ≤ D₂ + 1` and the `≤ 4Φ` step fails.  On `Unit` there\nis nothing to choose anyway. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_w_of_branches","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_w_of_branches","kind":"theorem","line":717,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothOperatorFamily.lean#L717-L748","doc":"**§13's two branches, packaged into `RothLemma4`'s `w`** — pure `ℚ` arithmetic.\n\n`index_le_or_sq_of_det_bound` returns `θ − t ≤ 2S/r ∨ (θ − t)²·d ≤ 2S` at `S = r·Φ`.  Both\nbranches give a `w` with `w ≤ Φ ∨ w² ≤ Φ` and `θ ≤ t + 2w`:\n\n* the first is `w := Φ`, once `2(rΦ)/r` is cancelled to `2Φ`;\n* the second is `w := max(0, (θ − t)/2)`, whose square is `(θ − t)²/4 ≤ Φ` by `r ≤ 2d`.\n\n⚠ `r ≤ 2d` is where `n ≤ r_m + 1` is spent — it is `r ≤ d + 1 ≤ 2d` for `d ≥ 1`, the same\nstep the source writes as `2/r_m ≤ 4/n`.  The `max(0, ·)` is what makes the second branch\nunconditional: at `θ < t` the halved difference is negative and `w := 0` serves. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_family_index_le_of_rothGrowth","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_family_index_le_of_rothGrowth","kind":"theorem","line":644,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothOperatorFamily.lean#L644-L715","doc":"**§13's conclusion with the realising family handed back.**\n\n`index_le_of_rothGrowth_of_factor_bound` below quantifies `hfac` over *every* bounded-order\nfamily, which is the shape a hypothesis wants but **not** a shape the recursion can discharge:\nthe induction hypothesis bounds the two Wronskians against `Θ'` for the system descended at the\nrepresentation length `r`, and `r` is not known until the representation exists.  A `∀ r`\nhypothesis would need a `Θ'` uniform in `r`, and there is none.\n\nSo this form runs `exists_sepW_ne_zero_le_degreeOf` first and returns the family, leaving the\nfactor bound as the *last* thing the caller supplies — by which time `r` is fixed and\n`rothGammaFull_descend` can be applied at it.  Everything else is the same derivation;\n`index_le_of_rothGrowth_of_factor_bound` is now a two-line corollary.\n\n⚠ The degree clauses are stated against `P`'s own `degreeOf`, not against `d₁`/`d₂`: that is\nwhat `SeparatedRepresentation` proves, and the caller has `P`'s degrees in hand. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_index_le_of_rothGrowth","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_index_le_of_rothGrowth","kind":"theorem","line":569,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothOperatorFamily.lean#L569-L603","doc":"**§13's conclusion with the row cost replaced by `1/ω`.**\n\nGiven the growth condition, an ordering in which the split-off variable comes last, and a\nnon-zero `P`, the index is at most `1/ω` plus §13's error term.\n\n§11 has since been read: `Γ_m`'s first clause is `RothGrowth` with `ω = 1/t`, so this is a\ntheorem about the source's condition, and under that identification the leading term is `t`\n— §13's own, on the nose.\n\n⚠ It used to read `2/ω`, and an earlier version of this docstring drew a *conclusion* from\nthat: \"`ω ≥ 2/ε` makes the row cost at most `ε` — this is what `ω(m, ε)` has to be\", which a\nlater pass had to retract as a conflation of the row cost with §14's height parameter `t/m`.\nThe retraction was right and the premise was still wrong: the `2` was never in §13 at all.\nCorrection 24 (`cost_sepW_le`) removed it.  ⚠ The lesson is the same one correction 17 taught\nabout labels — a wrong factor in a docstring is invisible to `lake build`, and here it had\nalready survived one round of *being reasoned about* without being checked against the source\nstep it was supposed to mirror. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothGrowth_split","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothGrowth_split","kind":"theorem","line":547,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothOperatorFamily.lean#L547-L555","doc":"The growth condition, read off for the split-off variable. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_degree_floor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_degree_floor","kind":"theorem","line":510,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothOperatorFamily.lean#L510-L545","doc":"The degree floor the join needs, packaged.\n\n§13 wants a **natural** `D₁` below every `d₁ k`; the growth condition supplies the\n**rational** bound `ω·d ≤ d₁ k`.  `⌈ω·d⌉` sits between them and also carries the cost bound.\nFactored out because both forms of the conclusion below need exactly this.\n\n⚠ The cost clause used to read `(dl + 1)/D₁ ≤ 2/ω`, and the factor `2` was `1 + 1/dl ≤ 2` —\nthe crude bound at `dl = 1`.  With `cost_sepW_le`'s numerator tightened from `r` to `r - 1`\nthe `+1` is no longer needed: `r - 1 ≤ dl`, and `dl/D₁ ≤ 1/ω` is exact, not crude.  This is\n§13's own step `(n-1)/r_{m-1} ≤ r_m/r_{m-1} ≤ t` verbatim. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_index_le_of_separated_le_degreeOf","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_index_le_of_separated_le_degreeOf","kind":"theorem","line":468,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothOperatorFamily.lean#L468-L507","doc":"**§13 with the row cost made explicit.**\n\nThe `t` slot is now `(d+1)/D₁` — the split-off variable's degree over the degree floor of the\n*other* variables — rather than the existentially-produced representation length.  That is the\nform the growth condition can act on: `RothGrowth` makes the split-off degree the smallest, so\n`D₁ ≥ ω·d` and `t ≲ 1/ω`.\n\n⚠ That last step is **not proved here**.  This theorem makes `t` a ratio of *degrees*; turning\nthat ratio into `1/ω` needs `RothGrowth` and is the remaining piece.  What is settled is that\n`t` no longer depends on how long a representation happens to be.\n\n⚠ The `t` slot is `d/D₁`, not `(d+1)/D₁`: `cost_sepW_le`'s numerator is `r - 1` and\n`r ≤ d + 1`, so the `+1` cancels against it rather than surviving into the bound.  That is\ncorrection 24 — see `cost_sepW_le`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_sepW_ne_zero_le_degreeOf","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_sepW_ne_zero_le_degreeOf","kind":"theorem","line":436,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothOperatorFamily.lean#L436-L466","doc":"`exists_sepW_ne_zero` with the length bound attached. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_index_le_of_separated","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_index_le_of_separated","kind":"theorem","line":387,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothOperatorFamily.lean#L387-L423","doc":"**§13 for an arbitrary non-zero polynomial in two variable groups** — every input\nsupplied, and no side condition beyond `q ≥ √(S/D₂)`.\n\nThe existence produces the representation length `r` and a bound `S` on the determinant's\nindex; the conclusion then holds for any `θ` and `q` meeting two conditions:\n\n* `r/D₁ ≤ θ` — the row cost `t` is below the index being bounded, so there is something to\n  bound.  (`index_le_two_mul_max_of_det_bound` needs `t ≤ θ`.)\n* `S ≤ q²·D₂` — `q` is a rational stand-in for `√(S/D₂)`, keeping everything in `ℚ`.\n\n⚠ An earlier version of this also demanded `S·D₂ ≤ r`.  That condition is **unnecessary** —\n§13's two branches are complementary, so the `max` form needs nothing — and it is **false** in\nthe recursion's regime, where it reads `Φ_m·r_m ≤ 1`.  See correction 9 in\n`QOU/Mathlib/RothInductionStep.lean`.\n\n⚠ What this is *not*: it is not Roth's lemma, and it does not choose `ω(m, ε)`.  It is §13's\ninequality with all of §13's inputs discharged. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.cost_sepW_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.cost_sepW_le","kind":"theorem","line":357,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothOperatorFamily.lean#L357-L385","doc":"The **column-graded** cost bound for a mixed operator family.\n\n`cost_bound_of_bounded_order` gives the *uniform* bound `r/D₁ + r/D₂`; this keeps the column\nindex, which is the whole difference between the linear conclusion the sixth correction\nrejected and §13's square root.\n\n⚠ **The twenty-fourth correction lives in the numerator `r - 1`.**  It used to be `r`, via\n`(Dx i).length ≤ i ≤ r` — true, but one larger than what is available, since `i : Fin r` gives\n`i ≤ r - 1`.  That single unit is the difference between the source's row cost and twice it:\n§13's row term is `(μ-1)/r_{m-1}` with `μ ≤ n`, i.e. numerator `n - 1 = r - 1`, and the chain\n`(r-1)/D₁ ≤ d/D₁ ≤ t` closes only with the tight numerator.  With `r` the chain has to go\nthrough `(d+1)/D₁ ≤ 2/ω`, and the factor `2` it picks up is not slack that later cancels — it\nis a `2t` where §14 needs a `t`, and `rothConstant m = 2^(m+1) - 3` is pinned against `t`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_index_le_of_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_index_le_of_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":346,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothOperatorFamily.lean#L346-L355","doc":"A non-zero polynomial's index is bounded by *some* rational — the form `hS` wants.\n\nThis is the only place the non-vanishing is used, and it is used for exactly one thing:\n`index d α 0 = ⊤` is below no `↑S`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_sepW_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_sepW_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":312,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothOperatorFamily.lean#L312-L344","doc":"**The separated Wronskian of a non-zero polynomial can be made non-zero.**\n\n⚠ Note the direction of the quantifier: it is not that *every* bounded-order family works —\nmost give a vanishing determinant — but that *some* does.  That is what\n`index_le_of_det_bound`'s `hS` needs, since a vanishing determinant has index `⊤` and\nsatisfies no bound at all. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_le_of_rothOpFamily_two_witness","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_le_of_rothOpFamily_two_witness","kind":"theorem","line":250,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothOperatorFamily.lean#L250-L290","doc":"**A non-degenerate instance.**  `σ = Fin 1`, unit degree, the origin, `P = X₀²`, and a\ngenuine `2 × 2` matrix with **distinct rows** and a **non-vanishing determinant**:\n\n    row lists  L = ![[], [x₀]]        (total orders 0 and 1, so N = 1)\n    matrix     ⎡ P    ∂P  ⎤  =  ⎡ X²    2X ⎤        det = 2X² − 4X² = −2X²\n               ⎣ ∂P   ∂²P ⎦     ⎣ 2X    2  ⎦\n\nso `S = 2`, and with `q = 3/2` (any rational with `q² ≥ 2`) the conclusion reads `2 ≤ 1 + 3`.\n\nWhy this one and not another `1 × 1`: here the **column cost genuinely grows** — column `0`\ncosts `0` in row `0` and column `1` costs `1` more — and the two rows are different, so the\ndeterminant is not forced to zero by repetition.  That is the configuration `index_det_ge`'s\npermutation step and `hc`'s `t + ν/rr` shape exist for.\n\n⚠ Still not exercised: the truncation.  `θ − t = 1` and `rr = 1`, so the cut-off `(θ−t)·rr`\nis `1` and lands inside a `2`-column matrix without clipping either column.  `max 0` never\nfires here; `RothArith.sum_max_truncation_active` is the guard that makes it fire. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_le_of_rothOpFamily_witness","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_le_of_rothOpFamily_witness","kind":"theorem","line":229,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothOperatorFamily.lean#L229-L248","doc":"**Satisfiability.**  One variable, unit degree, the origin, `P = X₀`, `n = 1`, empty row\nlist: `N = 0`, `D = 1`, `S = 1`, `q = 1`, and the conclusion reads `1 ≤ 0 + 2`.\n\n⚠ As with the other witnesses in this arc, this certifies that the hypotheses are **jointly\nsatisfiable** and nothing more.  With `n = 1` there is one column, so neither the truncation\nnor the growth of the column cost is exercised. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_le_of_rothOpFamily","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_le_of_rothOpFamily","kind":"theorem","line":199,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothOperatorFamily.lean#L199-L227","doc":"**§13, with the operator family supplied.**\n\nEvery hypothesis is now about the data (`d`, `z`, the row lists, `S`, `q`) rather than about\nan abstract `D` and an abstract cost bound — except `hS`, which is discussed below.\n\n`t` is `(N : ℚ)/D`: the rows have total order at most `N` and every variable has degree at\nleast `D`, which is Mahler's `∑_{h<m} j_{μh} ≤ μ−1` converted by `idxWeight_le_of_sum_le`.\n\n⚠ `hS` still asserts that the determinant's index is at most `S`, and that is **unsatisfiable\nwhen the determinant is zero** (`index d α 0 = ⊤`).  Nothing here proves the determinant is\nnon-zero; that is the bounded-order criterion's job and it is the arc's remaining input. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_rothOpFamily_of_idxWeight_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_rothOpFamily_of_idxWeight_le","kind":"theorem","line":182,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothOperatorFamily.lean#L182-L195","doc":"**The multi-index form**, recovered as a corollary.  Given row multi-indices of bounded\nweight, a family of the required shape exists.\n\nStated as an existence because `exists_multiIndexOf_eq` produces its list by choice; the\n`rothOpFamily` above stays free of that so concrete instances remain computable. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.cost_rothOpFamily_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.cost_rothOpFamily_le","kind":"theorem","line":169,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothOperatorFamily.lean#L169-L180","doc":"**`hc` discharged.**  A uniform bound `t` on the rows gives the per-column cost bound\n`t + ν/(d z)` that `index_le_of_det_bound` asks for.\n\nNote where the uniformity lives: `ht` quantifies over rows with a single `t`.  That is not an\nartefact of the statement — `sum_max_le_index_det` picks the permutation minimising the\ndiagonal sum, and may only do so because the bound does not depend on which row it lands in. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.cost_rothOpFamily","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.cost_rothOpFamily","kind":"theorem","line":163,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothOperatorFamily.lean#L163-L167","doc":"The cost splits: row part plus column part. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.sum_map_inv_replicate","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.sum_map_inv_replicate","kind":"theorem","line":157,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothOperatorFamily.lean#L157-L161","doc":"The cost of `ν` copies of one variable. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothOpFamily","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothOpFamily","kind":"def","line":152,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothOperatorFamily.lean#L152-L155","doc":"**§13's operator family.**  Row `μ` contributes the fixed list `L μ`; column `ν`\ncontributes `ν` copies of the distinguished variable `z`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.idxWeight_le_of_sum_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.idxWeight_le_of_sum_le","kind":"theorem","line":122,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothOperatorFamily.lean#L122-L148","doc":"A multi-index of total order at most `N`, on variables of degree at least `D`, has weight\nat most `N/D`.\n\nThis is what turns Mahler's `∑_{h<m} j_{μh} ≤ μ − 1` into a cost bound: the row constraint is\non the *total order*, and the degree floor converts it to a weight. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.sum_map_inv_eq_idxWeight","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.sum_map_inv_eq_idxWeight","kind":"theorem","line":112,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothOperatorFamily.lean#L112-L120","doc":"**The cost of an operator list is the weight of its multi-index.** ","signatureChunk":0}]}